\begin{abstract}
UKG-SE v3.0 is an autonomous, hardware-accelerated simulation architecture designed to
perform continuous self-evaluation and structural refinement across tensor-field
computations and Hamiltonian phase-space dynamics. The system integrates three core
subsystems: (1) a Solver Core that computes tensor fields $T^{\mu\nu}$ and evaluates
covariant residuals $R = \lVert \nabla_{\mu} T^{\mu\nu} \rVert$ for real-time mutation,
(2) a Hamiltonian Engine that models trajectories in phase space $(q,p)$ using
$H(q,p) = p^{2}/2 + V(q)$ and prunes unstable paths based on a divergence metric
$D = |q\,p|$, and (3) a Secure State Ledger that records all computational states,
refactoring events, and stability decisions using SHA-3 cryptographic hashing.

The UKG-SE v3.0 evolution loop enables the system to autonomously contract unstable
tensor pathways, eliminate divergent trajectories, and maintain a tamper-evident
record of its internal decision process. Experimental regimes demonstrate that
mutation and pruning frequencies are highly sensitive to stability thresholds,
highlighting the system's capacity for adaptive behavior under varying computational
constraints. UKG-SE v3.0 establishes a reproducible framework for autonomous
simulation engines capable of self-correction, stability-driven refinement, and
cryptographically verified provenance.
\end{abstract}